conversion gives \code{conversion_ratio} shares for the bond. A clean price of a call or a put gets the accrued interest on
its date added. A call with a \code{trigger} is a soft call: the issuer can call only where the share price is at least
\code{trigger} times the share price at which the redemption equals the conversion value. Not included: discrete
dividends, and stochastic rates.

The \code{delta} and \code{gamma} come from the first steps of the lattice, as for \code{\link[=compute_binomial_option_value]{compute_binomial_option_value()}}: a bump
of the spot much smaller than the lattice's spacing is noisy, since conversions and calls switch at nodes. The other
sensitivities are derivatives of the lattice by automatic differentiation; \code{credit_spread_sensitivity} is per unit of
spread (a basis point is 1e-4 of it). QuantLib returns the value only.

\strong{Difference from QuantLib 1.42.} QuantLib's time grid ends at \verb{(end / steps) * steps}, which in floating point can be
one unit in the last place below the exercise end; for an American conversion it then does not allow the conversion at
maturity, for some numbers of steps only (about 3\% of the cases tried). Here the bond can be converted at maturity.
The values are the same otherwise (relative 1e-11 on 291 of 300 cases tried); in the others they differ by up to 0.7\%,
in either direction, since the right to convert changes the probabilities that blend the discount rates.

\strong{Reset conversion price (a bond with a downward adjustment clause, MSCB).} \code{compute_reset_convertible_bond_value()} converts
at the price \verb{K(S) = min(upper, max(lower, ratio * S))} set by the share price \code{S} at the time of the conversion, for the
bond's face (its notional at \code{settlement_date}): the holder receives \verb{face / K(S)} shares, worth
\verb{face * max(S / upper, min(S / lower, 1 / ratio))}. No price is carried from one date to the next. With a ratio below 1 and an
American conversion, the holder converts at once whenever \verb{S >= lower / ratio}, for \verb{face / ratio} whatever the spot, so the
